1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,286
Coach: When two arrangements compete, compare them against the same questions.

2
00:00:04,286 --> 00:00:09,429
Coach: In this taster, will newcomers have an opportunity to discuss the text?

3
00:00:09,429 --> 00:00:12,857
Coach: How will they find out about the club?

4
00:00:12,857 --> 00:00:16,714
Coach: Can the stages and transitions fit within thirty minutes

5
00:00:16,714 --> 00:00:19,286
Coach: while preserving the minimum question time?

6
00:00:19,286 --> 00:00:24,000
Coach: These questions come from this task, not a universal ranking system.

7
00:00:24,350 --> 00:00:29,064
Coach: One candidate gives more room to a demonstration before pairs begin.

8
00:00:29,064 --> 00:00:34,636
Coach: Another keeps the example brief and leaves more room for participants to try.

9
00:00:34,636 --> 00:00:37,207
Coach: Neither description supplies exact durations yet.

10
00:00:37,207 --> 00:00:40,635
Coach: Compare the likely purpose and the possible cost,

11
00:00:40,635 --> 00:00:44,064
Coach: without treating those expectations as measured visitor outcomes.

12
00:00:44,414 --> 00:00:48,700
Coach: A longer example may make the reasoning easier to inspect.

13
00:00:48,700 --> 00:00:54,700
Coach: It can also use minutes that would otherwise go to pairs, sharing or questions.

14
00:00:54,700 --> 00:01:00,700
Coach: You cannot preserve every stage at full size simply by calling the arrangement balanced.

15
00:01:00,700 --> 00:01:06,271
Coach: The total stays the same even when each element has a good reason.

16
00:01:14,621 --> 00:01:19,335
Coach: A useful trade-off statement connects an arrangement to the goal

17
00:01:19,335 --> 00:01:21,049
Coach: and names its limitation.

18
00:01:21,049 --> 00:01:23,621
Coach: You can prefer the briefer example

19
00:01:23,621 --> 00:01:27,907
Coach: because the task asks newcomers to try discussing the text.

20
00:01:27,907 --> 00:01:33,049
Coach: That preference still needs a check that the example is clear enough

21
00:01:33,049 --> 00:01:37,335
Coach: and that the pair task has a useful starting point.

22
00:01:37,685 --> 00:01:44,542
You: I would keep the example brief so there is room to try the discussion in pairs.

23
00:01:44,542 --> 00:01:49,256
You: We still need to check that it shows the reasoning clearly.

24
00:01:49,606 --> 00:01:54,320
Coach: That statement does not claim a short example is always better.

25
00:01:54,320 --> 00:01:58,178
Coach: It explains why this candidate might fit this task.

26
00:01:58,178 --> 00:02:00,749
Coach: Someone can now question the link,

27
00:02:00,749 --> 00:02:07,178
Coach: suggest a clearer example or ask whether sharing needs a different place in the sequence.

28
00:02:07,178 --> 00:02:11,892
Coach: Those are useful revisions, not proof that the first speaker failed.

29
00:02:12,242 --> 00:02:17,385
Noah: I want the example to include a reason, not only an interpretation.

30
00:02:17,385 --> 00:02:22,099
Noah: Could we check that before deciding how brief it can be?

31
00:02:22,449 --> 00:02:27,592
Coach: That question helps define what must survive if the example is shortened.

32
00:02:27,592 --> 00:02:32,306
Coach: Keeping the reasoning visible is different from choosing the duration now.

33
00:02:32,306 --> 00:02:37,449
Coach: The group can look at a proposed example, check its essential content,

34
00:02:37,449 --> 00:02:41,306
Coach: and then consider how it affects the available time.

35
00:02:41,656 --> 00:02:45,085
Coach: Apply the same care to the question period.

36
00:02:45,085 --> 00:02:47,656
Coach: At least five minutes is required;

37
00:02:47,656 --> 00:02:51,942
Coach: it is not evidence that five minutes will answer everything.

38
00:02:51,942 --> 00:02:56,656
Coach: If questions need longer, another part must fit into less time.

39
00:02:56,656 --> 00:03:02,227
Coach: Preserve the requirement while being honest about what the information sheet can answer.

40
00:03:02,577 --> 00:03:06,863
Coach: Do not create a false choice between respect and selection.

41
00:03:06,863 --> 00:03:12,434
Coach: Taking someone's contribution seriously can mean preserving its purpose in a different form.

42
00:03:12,434 --> 00:03:17,577
Coach: It can also mean explaining why it does not fit this version.

43
00:03:17,577 --> 00:03:21,434
Coach: Respect does not require keeping every proposed detail unchanged.

44
00:03:21,784 --> 00:03:28,213
Coach: A trade-off remains open to review when you say which assumption it depends on.

45
00:03:28,213 --> 00:03:34,213
Coach: Here, a brief example depends on someone being able and willing to provide it.

46
00:03:34,213 --> 00:03:40,641
Coach: If that is not established, the next step is to check or revise the candidate,

47
00:03:40,641 --> 00:03:44,070
Coach: not to announce that the arrangement is ready.

48
00:03:56,420 --> 00:04:01,992
Coach: You can now explain a preference without presenting it as the group's decision.

49
00:04:01,992 --> 00:04:04,134
Coach: Keep the proposal, its purpose,

50
00:04:04,134 --> 00:04:07,563
Coach: its cost and its unresolved checks close together.

51
00:04:07,563 --> 00:04:14,849
Coach: A listener should not have to recover the caveat from a much earlier part of the discussion.

52
00:04:15,199 --> 00:04:18,628
Coach: Next, bring the candidate back to the group

53
00:04:18,628 --> 00:04:21,627
Coach: and ask about the support that matters.

54
00:04:21,627 --> 00:04:24,627
Coach: A useful check can reveal conditional support,

55
00:04:24,627 --> 00:04:28,484
Coach: a specific reservation or a need for more information.

56
00:04:28,484 --> 00:04:32,342
Coach: Each of those responses helps determine the next step

57
00:04:32,342 --> 00:04:35,770
Coach: without inventing a vote or a final authority.
